<p dir="ltr">Ask ten homeowners how long their renovation took, and you'll get ten different answers, which is exactly why "it depends" isn't good enough when you're planning your own. Whether undertaking comprehensive <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/residential-interior-design-london/">luxury residential interior design in London</a> or a phased restoration, here's a realistic breakdown of what actually shapes the timeline on a luxury London project.</p>

<h2 dir="ltr">How Long Does the Whole Process Take, Start to Finish?</h2>
<img class="wp-image-11925 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/interior-construction-work-in-progress.jpeg" alt="" width="1024" height="1024" />
<p dir="ltr">For a genuinely high-end renovation, one involving structural changes, an extension, or bespoke joinery, most projects run somewhere between nine and eighteen months from first brief to final handover. Consulting a comprehensive <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/full-house-renovation-guide/">full house renovation guide</a> helps clarify milestones, while balancing schedules alongside realistic <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/high-end-house-renovation-cost-london/">high-end house renovation costs in London</a>. A cosmetic refresh with no structural work can move far faster, but that's rarely what "luxury renovation" actually means once you factor in custom cabinetry, natural stone, and integrated technology.</p>

<h2 dir="ltr">How Much of That Time Is Design, and How Much Is Construction?</h2>
<img class="wp-image-11927 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/construction-work-in-a-home-at-knightsbridge.jpeg" alt="" width="1024" height="1024" />
<p dir="ltr">This is where most homeowners underestimate the schedule. A proper <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/space-planning/">space planning</a> and design phase, briefing, concept development, drawings, and material selection, typically takes two to four months on its own before a single wall comes down. Coordinating this early through <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/interior-architecture/">prime London interior architecture</a> ensures every partition and service route is locked in. As seen in our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/knightsbridge-residence-interior-design/">Knightsbridge luxury apartment case study</a>, meticulous preparation prevents mid-build changes and expensive re-orders.</p>

<h3 dir="ltr">Does Planning Permission Slow Things Down?</h3>
<img class="wp-image-11924 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/interior-construction-work.jpeg" alt="" width="1024" height="1024" />
<p dir="ltr">Significantly, if your project needs it. Internal reconfiguration rarely requires planning permission, but extensions, loft conversions, and anything touching a listed building or conservation area do. Understanding <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/permitted-development-rights-in-london-what-can-you-build-without-planning-permission/">permitted development rights in London</a> can determine whether statutory approval is necessary, where council sign-off alone can add eight to twelve weeks before construction even starts. Building this into your schedule from day one, rather than discovering it mid-project, is one of the simplest ways to protect your timeline.</p>

<h3 dir="ltr">Why Do Bespoke Finishes Add So Much Lead Time?</h3>
<img class="wp-image-11934 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/residential-interior-designed-for-a-client-from-Saudi-Arabia.jpeg" alt="" width="1024" height="1024" />
<p dir="ltr">Luxury finishes are, almost by definition, not sitting in a warehouse ready to ship. Hand-finished joinery, natural stone slabs, and specialist lighting can carry lead times of eight to sixteen weeks after the design is signed off. On complex schemes such as a <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/interior-designer-mayfair/">Mayfair luxury residential refurbishment</a>, ordering late is one of the most common reasons a "six-month" project quietly becomes a nine-month one.</p>

<h3 dir="ltr">What Actually Speeds Up a Luxury Renovation?</h3>
<img class="wp-image-11929 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/construction-work-at-a-residential-space.jpeg" alt="" width="1024" height="1024" />
<p dir="ltr">Sequencing. Projects run fastest when design, procurement, and construction sit under one team working from the same drawings, rather than being passed between separate consultants who each need time to interpret someone else's work. Evaluating <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/design-and-build-vs-separate-contractors-luxury-renovation/">design and build vs separate contractors in luxury renovation</a> shows why an integrated team reduces friction. A <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/design-and-build/">design and build</a> approach removes handoff delays almost entirely, backed by <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/360-degree-interior-design/">complete 360-degree interior design services</a> that synchronize trades with long-lead deliveries.</p>

<h3 dir="ltr">So What's a Realistic Timeline to Plan For?</h3>
<p dir="ltr">If your renovation involves structural work or an extension, plan for design and permissions to take three to five months, and construction a further six to twelve months depending on scale. Managing complexities on demanding heritage or modernist properties, as highlighted in our <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/barbican-residence-interior-design/">Barbican residential interior design case study</a>, requires building in a buffer of at least four to six weeks beyond that as standard practice.</p>
